Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.300
This is the symphony of the seas: an 1,188 feet long, 8,880 person, 18 deck, 7 neighborhood

00:00:08.349 --> 00:00:14.699
beast of a cruise ship that makes the Titanic look like a kayak. It cost $1.4 billion dollars

00:00:14.699 --> 00:00:18.930
but cruise ships only lasts about 25 years; 25 years of

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:24.959
constant cleaning and maintenance, plus an enormous amount of food, fuel, fees and employees.

00:00:24.959 --> 00:00:32.279
But tickets can cost just 60 or 70 dollars a day, cheaper than most retirement homes and a bit more fun

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:35.130
So making profit and quite a bit of it means

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:42.120
Optimizing everything from a ship's flag to its precise schedule. Like airlines, the business of cruising is one of low

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:47.340
Margins at extreme scale. Cruise Lines are experts at finding clever ways to save money

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:55.500
Norwegian cruises would even sail 1,100 miles into the middle of nowhere to avoid fines, they run a very tight ship

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:00.860
Cruises can be long, short, salty or not

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:06.510
But they all fall under three basic categories: Mainstream cruise lines optimized for scale

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:09.479
They sell lots of tickets or not a lot of money

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:14.490
Sometimes even at a loss. The average ticket sells for 1,293 dollars

00:01:14.619 --> 00:01:22.438
But cost the company even more in food fuel wages marketing and lots lots more. If everyone left their wallet at home,

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:28.949
These cruise lines would go out of business, but the average person spends four hundred and twenty nine dollars on board

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:33.780
They're the perfect captive audience. When your only choices are pepsi or sea water

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:36.000
They can pretty much guarantee your purchase

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.160
casinos, spas, shopping and drinks are key to making profit

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:45.899
And why you can't bring your own, and since everyone walks from cabin to dining room at least once a day

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:52.379
That's where they place lounges bars and casinos. There's no avoiding them. Premium wines are just the opposite

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:58.440
they make most of their money upfront. The no screaming kids on board tax means they can spend more time in ports

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:02.180
And Luxury ships may only hold a few hundred passengers

00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:05.260
But each pays more than enough to make the difference

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:13.960
All three categories serve very different markets so a good company catches them all which is why so many brands are owned by just two mega corporations

00:02:14.269 --> 00:02:20.889
Carnival owns Holland America princess piano and Costa even an airline at one point and Royal Caribbean on

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:23.979
Celebrity and Azamara. This way something like Costa, Concordia

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:28.690
Doesn't damage the rest of the company's reputation. When a ship reaches the end of its life

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:34.989
They can just hand it down to one of their other brands with lower standards, and a cruise ship is a vacation machine

00:02:34.989 --> 00:02:40.839
But it can never take a vacation November to April is when cold Americans escape to the Caribbean

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:47.769
But keeping that many ships there all year round would be extremely costly so is moving around empty ships from one region to another

00:02:48.049 --> 00:02:51.759
So they don't. when Caribbean ships switch to Europe in April

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:57.789
They sell repositioning tickets a one-way two-week cruise from Florida to say Amsterdam

00:02:57.790 --> 00:03:02.739
This way the ship is always making money and when one cruise ends the next begins

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:09.639
Turnaround day is a miracle of coordination. In less than half a day 6,000 people exit the ship and a hotel,

00:03:09.799 --> 00:03:14.828
restaurant, a theater, laundromat, and theme park clean and reset as if nothing ever happened

00:03:14.959 --> 00:03:22.209
When the ship arrives back at port early in the morning the clock starts ticking. While passengers sleep all their luggage must be carried ashore

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:28.389
Meanwhile trash is dumped, crew members switched, fuel pumped and a Costco of food and supplies

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:36.670
loaded onto the ship. 700,000 eggs, 3,000 gallons of soda and 15,000 pounds of potatoes. By 10:30

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:41.799
Passengers are gone and hundreds of housekeepers rushed to clean all 3,000 rooms

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:48.240
Then paperwork needs filed, maintenance performed, towel animals folded, and lunch cooked by Mr. Fieri.

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:51.660
All before new passengers arrive at one. By five o'clock

00:03:51.709 --> 00:03:56.199
The ship is on its way whether you're on it or not, because the sooner it reaches

00:03:56.389 --> 00:04:02.199
International waters the sooner the casino can start making Bank. Gambling is already very profitable

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:07.480
But especially when it's unregulated, so don't go overboard. Ocean water is pumped in

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:13.140
Desalinated and sent to rooms, which because surface area is so limited are mostly interiors

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:16.299
but the real moneymakers are suites and balconies, so

00:04:16.609 --> 00:04:23.739
Profitable that some ships have fake windows with virtual views and the least desirable lower decks are reserved for the crew

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:27.490
There's one for every 2 passengers, but you'll never see them off-duty

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:30.400
They work long hours for 3 to 12 months at a time

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:37.209
And they have to stay in their quarters when not working, and that's because a cruise ship can register in any country whose labor laws

00:04:37.209 --> 00:04:40.140
It prefers, most ships fly a flag of convenience

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:44.780
Usually Panama or Liberia saving millions in wages. Pretty handy

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:50.770
It's why you might have a Barbadian maitre d or latvian housekeeper, but never a pricey american

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:55.340
the only problem is the passenger vessel services act of 1886.

00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:58.780
It says no foreign vessel shall transport passengers between

00:04:58.970 --> 00:05:04.329
ports in the United States. Acruise can take passengers from America to another country and then

00:05:04.669 --> 00:05:07.239
Coincidentally those same people back to America

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:13.329
But never directly between American ports so short Pacific cruises often stop in Ensenada

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:19.030
It's only 50 miles from the US but on a 6000 passenger cruise it saves 1.8

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:20.150
million in fines

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:27.840
But for cruises to Hawaii they had to get creative. Every week Norwegian cruise ships going to Hawaii would take a four-day

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:34.860
1,135 mile detour to a tiny atoll with no running water or electricity called Tabuaeran

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:38.420
But because they technically step foot in the Republic of Kiribati

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:40.540
the trip is made 100% legal,

00:05:40.630 --> 00:05:46.149
And then Norwegian built the pride of America, because it's registered in the US it can avoid the detour

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:52.120
But it's subject to American labor laws, which is why many cruises to Hawaii are more expensive. Meanwhile

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:58.450
No port wants to be the next Tabuaeran so they fight to attract cruises. Cruise lines sell local activities for high

00:05:58.550 --> 00:06:01.660
Commissions and port fees are passed on to passengers

